{
 "title": "Midterm 2 · 3. Parallel RLC Circuits",
 "topic_keys": [
  "rlc_circuits",
  "second_order_response"
 ],
 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "**Note 3 of 3 · Textbook 8.1–8.3.** The parallel RLC circuit: natural response and step response. (8.4–8.5 series RLC is **not** on the midterm, so it is left out.)",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "d4a09385-855f-5159-9ecf-154a30d49873"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "With both $L$ and $C$, energy sloshes back and forth between them; $R$ is the friction. How much friction decides the shape of the response:",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "5a58de0c-cd3a-49db-81f7-fb001720cc20",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "a716989d-452f-4848-806f-375ae725f09e"
    }
   ],
   "id": "9e4a88d9-31a4-518d-b016-290b28e4cbee"
  },
  {
   "type": "list",
   "items": [
    "**Overdamped:** lots of friction. Creeps back to rest, no swinging.",
    "**Critically damped:** just enough friction. Fastest return without swinging.",
    "**Underdamped:** little friction. Swings past zero with shrinking swings."
   ],
   "ordered": false,
   "citations": [],
   "id": "417002d6-100e-5ec6-99ad-59d07c6d6dca"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "Every question is the same three moves: find the shape, find **two** starting values (two storage elements, so two), solve for two constants.",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "7c0d6e97-bf57-5cfb-a93b-d6140db39e1c"
  },
  {
   "type": "table",
   "columns": [
    "Topic",
    "What you are learning",
    "Where"
   ],
   "rows": [
    [
     "1. Which damping?",
     "$\\alpha$, $\\omega_0$, the roots, the $R$ for critical damping",
     "A"
    ],
    [
     "2. Natural response",
     "$v(t)$ from $v(0^+)$ and $\\dfrac{dv}{dt}(0^+)$",
     "B"
    ],
    [
     "3. Step response",
     "$i_L(t)$ when a DC current source turns on",
     "C"
    ]
   ],
   "citations": [],
   "id": "ed852496-474b-5fc6-8ac2-66c9818d0e2c"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "Every topic: what it is → the formula → a worked example → a **Try it**. ★ = from your lectures or Assessment Problems. Formula summary at the end; mixed questions in **Midterm 2 Practice Questions**.",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "6b2a80da-82ba-55af-8304-ecfd1e63d21f"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 1,
   "text": "Part A · Which damping?",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "5a58de0c-cd3a-49db-81f7-fb001720cc20",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "e21f79b4-9fc0-43d5-b233-9a1a88e53a8e"
    },
    {
     "source_id": "5a58de0c-cd3a-49db-81f7-fb001720cc20",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "712fc4cf-5239-4692-a920-5e6b2c82c627"
    },
    {
     "source_id": "d28cd690-29f8-4513-9501-34d2958f3a98",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "eecf7a6c-517f-4a25-b879-cd08d7baa98c"
    }
   ],
   "id": "b0f193b1-0236-5dec-8652-07b7979f4cec"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "Goal: from $R$, $L$, $C$, decide the shape and find the roots.",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "b4c22465-caff-55f1-9664-469cbd0c8c2e"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "You are given $R$, $L$ and $C$. Will the response swing?",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "a8e03173-787a-520d-9130-832f0fd97080"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "Compute two numbers. The **neper frequency** $\\alpha$ measures the friction. The **resonant frequency** $\\omega_0$ measures how fast it wants to swing.",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "957baace-4967-58d0-87d6-9d7b2175c611"
  },
  {
   "type": "equation",
   "latex": "\\alpha=\\frac{1}{2RC}",
   "display": true,
   "citations": [],
   "id": "2faa54bb-d7fc-5b4d-8657-d0aa59e45d05"
  },
  {
   "type": "equation",
   "latex": "\\omega_0=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{LC}}",
   "display": true,
   "citations": [],
   "id": "2994bcd1-5f29-520d-967d-8586642dc2d3"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "Then compare them:",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "c6f56aad-b316-5a42-a9af-a99fb11cbd73"
  },
  {
   "type": "table",
   "columns": [
    "Compare",
    "Shape",
    "Roots"
   ],
   "rows": [
    [
     "$\\alpha>\\omega_0$",
     "overdamped",
     "two real: $s_{1,2}=-\\alpha\\pm\\sqrt{\\alpha^2-\\omega_0^2}$"
    ],
    [
     "$\\alpha=\\omega_0$",
     "critically damped",
     "one repeated: $s=-\\alpha$"
    ],
    [
     "$\\alpha<\\omega_0$",
     "underdamped",
     "complex: $s=-\\alpha\\pm j\\omega_d$"
    ]
   ],
   "citations": [],
   "id": "f52fdac5-190d-51d7-8af6-ad9b1f5856f7"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "For the underdamped case, $\\omega_d$ is the swing frequency:",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "1207d34a-8bd8-5b1b-ac45-6b95576e6ed9"
  },
  {
   "type": "equation",
   "latex": "\\omega_d=\\sqrt{\\omega_0^2-\\alpha^2}",
   "display": true,
   "citations": [],
   "id": "693ef35c-57a9-5f87-8601-0726b99d36b2"
  },
  {
   "type": "worked_example",
   "title": "Example 1 · ★ Lecture example: three values of $R$",
   "prompt": "A parallel RLC circuit has $L=50\\,\\mathrm{mH}$ and $C=0.2\\,\\mu\\mathrm{F}$.\n\n(a) Find the roots for $R=200\\,\\Omega$. Which shape? (b) Repeat for $R=312.5\\,\\Omega$. (c) Which $R$ gives critical damping?",
   "steps": [
    {
     "explanation": "**$\\omega_0$** (same for every $R$).",
     "latex": "\\begin{gathered}\\omega_0^2=\\frac{1}{(0.05)(0.2\\times10^{-6})}=10^8 \\\\ \\omega_0=10\\,000\\ \\mathrm{rad/s}\\end{gathered}"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**(a) $\\alpha$** for $200\\,\\Omega$. It is bigger than $\\omega_0$: overdamped.",
     "latex": "\\begin{gathered}\\alpha=\\frac{1}{2(200)(0.2\\times10^{-6})}=12\\,500 \\\\ s_{1,2}=-12\\,500\\pm\\sqrt{12\\,500^2-10^8}=-12\\,500\\pm7500 \\\\ s_1=-5000,\\qquad s_2=-20\\,000\\ \\mathrm{rad/s}\\end{gathered}"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**(b) $\\alpha$** for $312.5\\,\\Omega$. Now it is smaller than $\\omega_0$: underdamped.",
     "latex": "\\begin{gathered}\\alpha=\\frac{1}{2(312.5)(0.2\\times10^{-6})}=8000 \\\\ \\omega_d=\\sqrt{10^8-8000^2}=6000 \\\\ s_{1,2}=-8000\\pm j6000\\ \\mathrm{rad/s}\\end{gathered}"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**(c) Critical:** set $\\alpha=\\omega_0$ and solve for $R$.",
     "latex": "\\begin{gathered}\\frac{1}{2R(0.2\\times10^{-6})}=10\\,000 \\\\ R=250\\,\\Omega\\end{gathered}"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "(a) $-5000$, $-20\\,000\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}$, overdamped (b) $-8000\\pm j6000\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}$, underdamped (c) $250\\,\\Omega$",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "5a58de0c-cd3a-49db-81f7-fb001720cc20",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "f3931095-9e20-459d-9547-25aabdc4524e"
    }
   ],
   "id": "1976aad8-313b-59e2-a3bc-fef3a7d46fcc"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "tone": "warning",
   "text": "In a **parallel** RLC, a bigger $R$ means a **smaller** $\\alpha=\\dfrac{1}{2RC}$: less damping, more swinging. (Feels backwards: a big parallel $R$ draws almost no current, so it barely brakes anything.)",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "f970fb83-84af-5bf8-ae42-fa5d13e3924c"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "tone": "tip",
   "text": "**Shortcut: $R$ for critical damping** (set $\\alpha=\\omega_0$ and solve once):\n\n$$\nR=\\frac12\\sqrt{\\frac LC}\n$$\n\nExample: $\\dfrac12\\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.05}{0.2\\times10^{-6}}}=\\dfrac12(500)=250\\,\\Omega$, as above.",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "7ccc4093-ab40-5bff-b407-f32cc0060387"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "Slide $R$ from $100\\,\\Omega$ up. At which $R$ does the curve start to swing below zero and back?\n\n```graph\ntitle \"Parallel RLC: $v(t)$ for $L=50\\,\\mathrm{mH}$, $C=0.2\\,\\mu\\mathrm{F}$, $v(0)=0$, $i_L(0)=-12\\,\\mathrm{mA}$\"\nx 0 1.5 label=\"$t$ (ms)\"\ny -5 7 label=\"$v$ (V)\"\nslider R 100 600 150 step=10 label=\"$R$ ($\\Omega$)\"\nlet C \"2/10000000\"\nlet L \"0.05\"\nlet a \"1/(2*R*C)\"\nlet w \"1/sqrt(L*C)\"\nlet d \"a^2-w^2\"\nlet q \"sqrt(abs(d))\"\nlet D0 \"0.012/C\"\ncurve \"if(d>1000000, (D0/(2*q))*(exp((-a+q)*x/1000)-exp((-a-q)*x/1000)), if(d<-1000000, (D0/q)*exp(-a*x/1000)*sin(q*x/1000), D0*(x/1000)*exp(-a*x/1000)))\" color=1 label=\"$v(t)$\"\nhline \"0\"\nreadout \"$\\alpha$\" \"a\" unit=\" rad/s\"\nreadout \"$\\omega_0$\" \"w\" unit=\" rad/s\"\n```",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "bd7ae222-0835-5e06-89a4-113e4700d6c9"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "Up to $250\\,\\Omega$ the curve rises once and dies out (overdamped, then critically damped at exactly $250\\,\\Omega$). Above $250\\,\\Omega$, $\\alpha<\\omega_0$ and it swings (underdamped).",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "5a772709-610a-5ca1-a194-6026bff7a7e3"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "tone": "warning",
   "text": "\"No swinging\" does not mean \"never crosses zero\". With other starting values an overdamped $v$ can cross zero **once** (the first example in Part B does). Only the underdamped shape crosses again and again.",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "fb042494-75eb-52b2-a292-65cf82f9a05e"
  },
  {
   "type": "practice_problem",
   "title": "Try it 1 · ★ AP 8.1: roots and critical $R$",
   "difficulty": "introductory",
   "origin": "source",
   "prompt": "A parallel RLC circuit has $R=1\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}$, $L=12.5\\,\\mathrm{H}$, $C=2\\,\\mu\\mathrm{F}$. (a) Find the roots. (b) Which shape? (c) Find the roots for $R=1562.5\\,\\Omega$. (d) Which $R$ gives critical damping?",
   "solution": "",
   "steps": [
    {
     "explanation": "**$\\alpha$ and $\\omega_0^2$.**",
     "latex": "\\begin{gathered}\\alpha=\\frac{1}{2(1000)(2\\times10^{-6})}=250 \\\\ \\omega_0^2=\\frac{1}{(12.5)(2\\times10^{-6})}=40\\,000\\quad(\\omega_0=200)\\end{gathered}"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**(a, b) Roots.** $\\alpha>\\omega_0$: overdamped.",
     "latex": "\\begin{gathered}s_{1,2}=-250\\pm\\sqrt{250^2-40\\,000}=-250\\pm150 \\\\ s_1=-100,\\qquad s_2=-400\\ \\mathrm{rad/s}\\end{gathered}"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**(c)** New $\\alpha$, now below $\\omega_0$.",
     "latex": "\\begin{gathered}\\alpha=\\frac{1}{2(1562.5)(2\\times10^{-6})}=160 \\\\ s_{1,2}=-160\\pm j\\sqrt{40\\,000-160^2}=-160\\pm j120\\ \\mathrm{rad/s}\\end{gathered}"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**(d)** Set $\\alpha=\\omega_0=200$.",
     "latex": "R=\\frac{1}{2(200)(2\\times10^{-6})}=1250\\,\\Omega"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "(a) $-100$, $-400\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}$ (b) overdamped (c) $-160\\pm j120\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}$ (d) $1250\\,\\Omega$",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "fac249fc-9f9d-4aef-9d15-3a9cdec0cb3c",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "1be30af1-cad8-4b93-a204-19a26684f1c1"
    }
   ],
   "id": "2f664b4c-c234-5e15-8ba2-4329948748e2"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 1,
   "text": "Part B · Natural response",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "5a58de0c-cd3a-49db-81f7-fb001720cc20",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "7de1a92d-fbac-40d7-9999-9365e3fffd04"
    },
    {
     "source_id": "5a58de0c-cd3a-49db-81f7-fb001720cc20",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "560744e7-4e40-4658-958c-cfb0c4da8470"
    },
    {
     "source_id": "5a58de0c-cd3a-49db-81f7-fb001720cc20",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "70cd21a0-a2cc-4787-8701-054d4ea1b0f5"
    },
    {
     "source_id": "d28cd690-29f8-4513-9501-34d2958f3a98",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "ea9f4d33-f701-4b24-b6c2-b25983006ca8"
    },
    {
     "source_id": "d28cd690-29f8-4513-9501-34d2958f3a98",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "7d9125be-343a-4cb5-9135-2da606a39d96"
    },
    {
     "source_id": "d28cd690-29f8-4513-9501-34d2958f3a98",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "b573b578-7fa9-4f3e-a81b-676dacfb6564"
    }
   ],
   "id": "a4880785-a129-536b-9d34-ed5df8dd19d5"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "Goal: the capacitor is charged and the inductor carries current. At $t=0$ they are left alone with $R$. Find $v(t)$.",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "0ae9c0b2-0ce1-5384-b3fb-bbb4a2de9b80"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "In a parallel circuit all three elements share **one** voltage $v$. So solve for $v$ first; every branch current follows from it.",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "4e20026a-2637-5cb4-a88d-05c174e7cb0d"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "**Each shape has its own formula** with two unknown constants:",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "2519345a-c099-598f-af1e-90dc86f4903b"
  },
  {
   "type": "table",
   "columns": [
    "Shape",
    "$v(t)$"
   ],
   "rows": [
    [
     "overdamped",
     "$A_1e^{s_1t}+A_2e^{s_2t}$"
    ],
    [
     "critically damped",
     "$D_1te^{-\\alpha t}+D_2e^{-\\alpha t}$"
    ],
    [
     "underdamped",
     "$B_1e^{-\\alpha t}\\cos\\omega_dt+B_2e^{-\\alpha t}\\sin\\omega_dt$"
    ]
   ],
   "citations": [],
   "id": "7ed26b07-1f03-5572-aed8-996364fb4bc6"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "You find the two constants from two starting values: $v(0^+)$ and the starting slope $\\dfrac{dv}{dt}(0^+)$.",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "310cc1c6-6b2d-5524-9609-9dab4569590f"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "**The starting slope** is the one new move. The capacitor rule $i_C=C\\dfrac{dv}{dt}$ gives it, once you know $i_C(0^+)$. Get that from KCL at the top node (all three branch currents pointing down):",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "96041aaa-03a9-563b-a71c-eb836792756b"
  },
  {
   "type": "equation",
   "latex": "\\begin{gathered}i_C(0^+)=-\\,i_L(0^+)-\\frac{v(0^+)}{R} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt}(0^+)=\\frac{i_C(0^+)}{C}\\end{gathered}",
   "display": true,
   "citations": [],
   "id": "e9ddfa6a-e2e3-55b5-a91b-93636351433a"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "The recipe, with the same words every time:",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "b191a2b2-f63b-5966-8d23-d2a9fc760cce"
  },
  {
   "type": "table",
   "columns": [
    "Step",
    "Do this"
   ],
   "rows": [
    [
     "1. **Shape**",
     "$\\alpha$, $\\omega_0$: which case, and the roots"
    ],
    [
     "2. **Start**",
     "$v(0^+)=V_0$ and $\\dfrac{dv}{dt}(0^+)=\\dfrac{i_C(0^+)}{C}$"
    ],
    [
     "3. **Constants**",
     "Two equations, two unknowns (below)"
    ],
    [
     "4. **Plug in**",
     "Constants into the formula"
    ]
   ],
   "citations": [],
   "id": "094377f8-3c10-5305-b721-80055e4216e9"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "Step 3 always uses $t=0$ in $v$ and in $\\dfrac{dv}{dt}$. Each case gives its own pair of equations:",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "d0b79e7b-17ae-57f0-93d3-25d080c17f98"
  },
  {
   "type": "solution",
   "title": "Overdamped constants, with an example",
   "text": "Set $t=0$ in $v$ and in its derivative:\n\n$$\nv(0^+)=A_1+A_2\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}(0^+)=s_1A_1+s_2A_2\n$$\n\nExample: $s_1=-5000$, $s_2=-20\\,000$, $v(0^+)=12$, $\\dfrac{dv}{dt}(0^+)=-450\\,000$. Solve the pair:\n\n$$\nA_1+A_2=12\n$$\n\n$$\n-5000A_1-20\\,000A_2=-450\\,000\n$$\n\n$$\nA_1=-14,\\qquad A_2=26\n$$",
   "collapsed": true,
   "citations": [],
   "id": "c8a630e6-6ba8-5ebb-8fda-938d142aae19"
  },
  {
   "type": "solution",
   "title": "Critically damped constants, with an example",
   "text": "At $t=0$ the $t\\,e^{-\\alpha t}$ term is $0$, so $D_2$ comes for free:\n\n$$\nv(0^+)=D_2\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}(0^+)=D_1-\\alpha D_2\n$$\n\nExample: $\\alpha=10\\,000$, $v(0^+)=12$, $\\dfrac{dv}{dt}(0^+)=-390\\,000$.\n\n$$\nD_2=12\n$$\n\n$$\nD_1=-390\\,000+10\\,000(12)=-270\\,000\n$$",
   "collapsed": true,
   "citations": [],
   "id": "476084ac-1bbc-57c1-8cc4-ddb9dae3e757"
  },
  {
   "type": "solution",
   "title": "Underdamped constants, with an example",
   "text": "At $t=0$, $\\cos=1$ and $\\sin=0$, so $B_1$ comes for free:\n\n$$\nv(0^+)=B_1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}(0^+)=-\\alpha B_1+\\omega_dB_2\n$$\n\nExample: $\\alpha=200$, $\\omega_d=979.8$, $v(0^+)=0$, $\\dfrac{dv}{dt}(0^+)=98\\,000$.\n\n$$\nB_1=0\n$$\n\n$$\nB_2=\\frac{98\\,000}{979.8}=100.02\n$$",
   "collapsed": true,
   "citations": [],
   "id": "428b1897-cf3f-509a-bb0f-f1722b4fe37b"
  },
  {
   "type": "worked_example",
   "title": "Example 2 · ★ Lecture example: overdamped",
   "prompt": "For the circuit below, $v(0^+)=12\\,\\mathrm{V}$ and $i_L(0^+)=30\\,\\mathrm{mA}$.\n\n```schematic\nC1 0,3 -> 0,0 \"$0.2\\,\\mu\\mathrm{F}$\" i=\"$i_C$\" v=\"$v$\"\nwire 0,3 -> 3,3 -> 6,3\nL1 3,3 -> 3,0 \"$50\\,\\mathrm{mH}$\" i=\"$i_L$\"\nR1 6,3 -> 6,0 \"$200\\,\\Omega$\" i=\"$i_R$\"\nwire 6,0 -> 0,0\n```\n\n(a) Find the starting current in each branch. (b) Find $\\dfrac{dv}{dt}(0^+)$. (c) Find $v(t)$.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "explanation": "**Shape** (from Part A): overdamped.",
     "latex": "s_1=-5000,\\qquad s_2=-20\\,000\\ \\mathrm{rad/s}"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Start (a):** $i_L$ is given, $i_R$ from Ohm\\'s law, $i_C$ from KCL.",
     "latex": "\\begin{gathered}i_L(0^+)=30\\,\\mathrm{mA},\\qquad i_R(0^+)=\\frac{12}{200}=60\\,\\mathrm{mA} \\\\ i_C(0^+)=-30-60=-90\\,\\mathrm{mA}\\end{gathered}"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Start (b):** the slope.",
     "latex": "\\frac{dv}{dt}(0^+)=\\frac{-0.09}{0.2\\times10^{-6}}=-450\\,000\\ \\mathrm{V/s}"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Constants.**",
     "latex": "\\begin{gathered}A_1+A_2=12 \\\\ -5000A_1-20\\,000A_2=-450\\,000 \\\\ A_1=-14,\\qquad A_2=26\\end{gathered}"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Plug in (c).**",
     "latex": "v(t)=-14e^{-5000t}+26e^{-20\\,000t}\\ \\mathrm{V},\\qquad t\\ge0"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "(a) $i_L=30$, $i_R=60$, $i_C=-90\\,\\mathrm{mA}$ (b) $-450\\,000\\,\\mathrm{V/s}$ (c) $v=-14e^{-5000t}+26e^{-20\\,000t}\\,\\mathrm{V}$",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "5a58de0c-cd3a-49db-81f7-fb001720cc20",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "a4b67084-4e39-48c3-86ee-69b36f79dafb"
    }
   ],
   "id": "69e20cb5-c18c-5b76-8b90-9dc2c4509a35"
  },
  {
